# Draft:Expedition to Prussia (1678–1679)

}}
The Prussian expedition; .}} (24 October 1678 – 13 February 1679) refers to the failed Swedish invasion of Ducal Prussia, then part of Brandenburg–Prussia, from Swedish Livonia in late 1678 during the Scanian War.
Reminder to elaborate
It was devised to relieve Swedish Pomerania, much of which had been occupied by Brandenburg by 1678. King Charles XI appointed Field Marshal Henrik Horn in late 1678 to command the expedition. Horn subsequently mustered over 11,400 men in Livonia and marched towards Ducal Prussia through Lithuanian territory, reaching the town of Tilsit in December.
Promised Polish support, however, never materialized for the Swedes, and Horn was ultimately driven back by Elector Frederick William in the Great Sleigh Drive. The Swedes returned to Riga in early 1679, having suffered losses totalling 6,500 men throughout the expedition.
Background
French pressure on a reluctant Sweden to honor the Treaty of Stockholm (1672) led to the Swedish invasion of Brandenburg in late 1674. The invasion—however, culminated in defeat at the Battle of Fehrbellin in June 1675—which forced the Swedish army to abandon the campaign entirely.
The defeat at Fehrbellin had disastrous consequences; by mid-1675, the Holy Roman Empire, the Dutch Republic, and Denmark–Norway had all declared war on Sweden, expanding the conflict into the Scanian War. The subsequent Danish and Brandenburger offensive into Swedish Pomerania left Sweden with only Stralsund and Rügen to defend, while a Norwegian offensive into Bohuslän was driven back.
Plans for an incursion into Ducal Prussia had already been devised by 1676;
Prelude
To lead the expedition, Charles XI appointed Field Marshal . After much delay, Horn finally arrived in Riga in February 1678, where 6,091 men had been mustered. Horn, however, died on the 17th—delaying the expedition again.
Expedition
1678
The Swedish army assembled at Holmhoff—on the left bank of the Düna River (Daugava)—on 20 October. Horn crossed the Bolder Aa River (Lielupe) on the 24th—rendezvousing at Schlock (Sloka) on the 25th. Yet, Stralsund had already fallen to Frederick William the same day; the expedition had begun too late. Even so, the advance proceeded (as Horn was not aware until late November) along the coast; Tuckum (Tukums) was reached on the 26th, and Schrunden (Skrunda) in the following days. It was not until 11 November that the army reached the Heiligen Aa River (Svete), which marked the border between Courland and Lithuania. The march had been slow—mainly owing to the poor roads and weather.
1679
Aftermath
Charles XI did not learn of the retreat until March. Yet he immediately prepared another 8,000 men to be sent to Livonia, while Lilliehöök was ordered to resume negotiations with Sobieski for renewed attack.
